            <ArticleTitle>Catastrophic cement reaction following cementation for megaprosthesis for proximal femoral fracture.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr-2017-220158</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2017-220158</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bone cement implantation syndrome (BCIS) is a well-described and potentially fatal complication of orthopaedic surgery involving pressurised bone cement. Although also described for certain spinal procedures, it is most commonly associated with cemented hip and knee arthroplasty and with cemented hemiarthroplasty following neck of femur fracture in particular.Donaldson et alproposed the definition of BCIS as a syndrome &quot;characterized by hypoxia, hypotension or both and/or unexpected loss of consciousness occurring around the time of cementation, prosthesis insertion, reduction of the joint or, occasionally, limb tourniquet deflation in a patient undergoing cemented bone surgery&quot;. Other features include increased vascular resistance, cardiac arrhythmias and cardiac arrest post cement use.We describe a case of a patient who suffered a catastrophic reaction to cement during surgery for a comminuted proximal femoral fracture.</AbstractText>
